WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.303 align:start position:0%
- Wall Street realized they
could go from being the bad guys

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:06.006
after the '08 crisis
to being the good guys

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.341
if they just said
the right things,

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:11.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
muse about diversity
and inclusion,

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:13.079
unjust impact of climate change

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.382
after you fly in a
private jet to Davos,

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:17.317
it's pretty good work
if you can get it.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was a cynical
arranged marriage though,

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:22.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
between two partners
in that marriage

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:25.025 align:start size:97% position:3%
that did not particularly
love or respect one another,

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.028 align:start size:97% position:3%
big business and the
Neo-progressive woke movement.

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:31.498 align:start size:97% position:3%
But together, they were each
able to get something out

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:32.699
of the trade.

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:34.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was more like
mutual prostitution.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.804
And that was what led to
the birth of Woke Inc.

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:40.073
It wasn't just woke, and
it wasn't just the Inc,

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.008
it was both of 'em.

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:44.711
(dramatic music)

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:51.518
- I'm Dave Rubin and joining
me today is the co-founder

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:53.486
of Strive Asset Management

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:56.022
as well as the author
of the brand new book,

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:59.025
Nation of Victims,
Identity Politics,

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:02.462
The Death of Merit, and the
Path Back to Excellence,

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:03.863
Vivek Ramaswamy.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.599 align:start size:84% position:16%
Welcome, finally,
to the Ruben Report.

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:08.501
- It's good to talk to
you, man. How are you?

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.003
- I am doing well.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:12.472
I have wanted to have you
on for quite some time.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.340
And then about, I don't know,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.842
it was about four
or five months ago,

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:20.346
I was hosting a PragerU event

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:24.017
in West Palm and you were
one of the keynote speakers

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:25.552
and you actually blew me away.

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:27.387 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're a phenomenal
public speaker,

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:29.589 align:start size:88% position:13%
great way of connecting
with the audience,

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:30.790
talking about all the things

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.759 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I'm always
yammering on about.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:36.162 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that's what I wanna dive
into on the show today.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:38.431
But before we get into business

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.233
and why you're doing
this and the book,

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.969
give me a little Vivek 101,

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:44.270
little backstory of little Vivek

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.439
and what got him to
be this power player

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:48.808
in business and culture.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:50.276
- Yeah, I don't know.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.112 align:start size:88% position:13%
I can't, you know,
promise all the answers,

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I can tell you a
little bit about my story.

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.082
You know, I grew up
at Cincinnati, Ohio.

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:58.651 align:start size:84% position:16%
My parents were
immigrants from India.

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:01.087
My dad came over in
the late seventies,

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.056
my mom in the early eighties.

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:05.091 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, you know, we
would frequently joke

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:07.527
around about why on earth you
came halfway around the world

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:09.596
to southwest Ohio.

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:12.432
And my dad's reason for coming
was that his older sister was

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:13.867
in Fort Wayne, Indiana,

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:16.703 align:start position:0%
which of course begged the
question of why she came halfway

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:18.538
around the world to
Fort Wayne, Indiana.

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:21.975 align:start size:97% position:3%
And our family joke is that
it is the only US state

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:25.478 align:start position:0%
with the word "India" contained
in the name of the state.

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:27.013
So that gives you a sense
of what the upbringing was,

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.815
corny jokes in the Midwest.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.850
And that was, you know,
the immigrant upbringing

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.185 align:start size:88% position:13%
which I could, you know,
tell you all about.

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:34.354
Some of that's in
the book actually,

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:37.056
but anyway, I ended up
going into science first.

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.025 align:start size:94% position:6%
I went to Harvard studying
molecular biology,

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:40.927 align:start size:84% position:16%
thought I was gonna
be a scientist,

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.196
ended up in the world
of biotech investing,

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:46.933 align:start size:94% position:6%
which I did for seven
years, from 2007 to 2014.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:49.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
About three years in I
had this itch to study law

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.271
and political philosophy.

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:53.973 align:start size:84% position:16%
I had been such a
science centric guy
that I told my bosses

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.009 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I was gonna leave
my hedge fund job

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.144
and go to law school.

00:02:58.144 --> 00:02:59.112
I had a seat at Yale.

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:00.747
They said, keep your job,

00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:03.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
keep managing a
portfolio if you want.

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.751
I said that sounds great.

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:08.788
So I did that for three
years, from 2010 to 2013.

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:10.056
When I graduated,

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:12.325
I no longer had law
school on my docket.

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:13.993
So I used the extra time

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:17.263 align:start size:97% position:3%
for an ignominious short lived
stint as a standup comedian

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.665
in New York city.

00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:21.868
That did not work out
well. It was humbling.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:22.802
And ultimately-

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:23.770
- (laughs) Wait. What year?

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:24.837
What year was that?

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:27.674
- That was 2013 and early 2014.

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:30.610
So 2013 was when
I did my 10 shows.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.046 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I wonder man if we
crossed paths at that time,

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:35.815
that was like right around
when I was in the thick of it

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:37.217
over there.

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:38.218 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And do you remember
- Were you in comedy as well?

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:39.219
what clubs you were doing?

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.720
Oh, yeah. I used
to be funny man.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.121
Long time ago.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:43.690 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Okay, so I did like
Broadway Comedy Club, Gotham.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.058
- Yeah?

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.192
- A couple other places, yeah.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:47.260
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, woo.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That's pretty funny.
- 10 shows

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:49.963
and then you're retired, huh?

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:50.964
- Yeah, and then
I hung the jersey

00:03:50.964 --> 00:03:52.732
on that due to the better advice

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.935 align:start size:97% position:3%
of friends and family
(laughs) who were, you know,

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:58.104
very honest audience members.

00:03:58.104 --> 00:04:00.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
But anyway, then I,
yeah, it's a long story,

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.642 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it led me to the doorstep
of founding my company,

00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:05.278 align:start size:88% position:13%
which was a biotech
company that, you know,

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:08.381 align:start size:97% position:3%
all the things that I saw
from my career as an investor

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:10.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
that frustrated me as an
outside investor in biotech,

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.218
I wanted to fix.

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:13.786
Decided to do it by
starting a company.

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:18.791
And so built the company, led
it as CEO from 2014 to 2021,

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:20.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
we developed a
number of medicines.

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:21.594
Some of them failed.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:22.829
Some of them worked.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:24.464
Five of them are FDA
proof products today,

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:26.232
which I'm incredibly proud of.

00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:27.600
But at the end of the day,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.337 align:start size:84% position:16%
I ended up having
to step down in 2021

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:34.707
to focus on what I
saw as a new cancer,

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:38.878
a cultural cancer that
threatened the very dream

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.947
that allowed me to
achieve everything I
ever had in my life.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:43.216
And, you know, look, I was
worried about this new trend,

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:46.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
about the merger of corporate
power and state power

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:49.555
to advance political agendas
through the private sector.

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:51.791 align:start size:97% position:3%
I had some of those
firsthand experiences myself

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:53.726
in my experience as a CEO

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:55.228 align:start size:88% position:13%
of a multi-billion
dollar biotech company.

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:58.064
There were some demands
that came with it that I,

00:04:58.064 --> 00:05:01.334 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, that I thought were
threatening to the system

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.569 align:start size:84% position:16%
of American capitalism
and democracy.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:06.139
And so that led me to step
aside to write my first book,

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:07.307
which was Woke Inc,

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:09.475 align:start size:88% position:13%
which I think you
might be familiar with.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:11.511
I published that book enjoyed

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:13.479
for a year and a half
traveling the country.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:16.816 align:start size:88% position:13%
No agenda, just
speaking my mind freely

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.284
without any corporate agenda,

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:20.987
any political agenda,
really enjoyed that,

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:22.655 align:start size:84% position:16%
that led to my
second book as well,

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:24.223
which was a sequel to Woke Inc.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:26.826 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's the one that
we're talking about now.

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:30.363
But I decided that I wasn't
gonna just change the world

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:31.631
by writing books.

00:05:31.631 --> 00:05:33.232
I needed to do something

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:35.034
about the problems
I was describing,

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:38.071 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's what led me also
more recently to found Strive,

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:41.140 align:start position:0%
which is our new firm competing
directly against BlackRock.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.742
So, that's sort
- That's it.

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:44.677 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the life career
journey in a nutshell.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.079
- All right, you gave me a
lot of avenues to go down.

00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:47.246
So let's back up for a second

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:48.514 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause obviously I
wanna talk about Strive

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:49.749
and I wanna talk
about the new book.

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:52.118
But Woke Inc., I mean,
basically, you know,

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:55.121
you were laying out
that this woke thing

00:05:55.121 --> 00:05:56.689
that we were all seeing,

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:58.091 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's going going through
the universities,

00:05:58.091 --> 00:06:00.760 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's on television everywhere,
you turn on Apple TV,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:04.330
that it's not just sort of
this organic thing coming

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.065
from kids off Tumblr,

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:10.303
that there actually
is an organizational
ideology behind it.

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:11.804
So can you talk about
that a little bit?

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:14.107
'cause people always ask
me either when I do QnA's

00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:15.975
on the show where
I go out publicly.

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:17.477
Where is it coming from?

00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:19.178
What is driving it truly?

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.548
- Yeah, so I mean, look,

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:24.384
that took a whole separate
book length work to describe,

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:25.818
but one of the things I said

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:28.454 align:start size:97% position:3%
in that book was that it
when wokeness met capitalism,

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:30.289
that it actually
became unstoppable.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:33.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was supercharged
with the dollars that flow

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:36.462
through our system of free
market American capitalism.

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:39.799 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's when it really
became not just a challenge

00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:42.602
to the system, it
became the system.

00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:44.137
And it happened in the aftermath

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:45.738
of the 2008 financial crisis.

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:47.206
And I saw this firsthand, right.

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:49.442
I, you know, got my first
job at that hedge fund

00:06:49.442 --> 00:06:51.377
in New York city
in the fall of 2007

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:53.746
on the eve of the '08 crisis.

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:56.082
And the long story short,
and it is a long story,

00:06:56.082 --> 00:06:57.450
and it was, you know,
it was my last book.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.419
So we could go on for
hours about this one.

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:00.887
But the short version

00:07:00.887 --> 00:07:04.724
of it is that Wall Street
realized they could go

00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:07.260
from being the bad guys
after the '08 crisis

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.030 align:start size:97% position:3%
to being the good guys if they
just said the right things,

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.000 align:start size:84% position:16%
muse about diversity
and inclusion,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.768
unjust impact of climate change

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:18.070
after you fly in a
private jet to Davos.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.006
It's pretty good work
if you can get it.

00:07:20.006 --> 00:07:22.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was a cynical
arranged marriage though,

00:07:22.008 --> 00:07:25.445 align:start size:84% position:16%
between two partners
in that marriage

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:27.713 align:start size:97% position:3%
that did not particularly
love or respect one another,

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:30.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
big business and the
Neo-progressive woke movement.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:34.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
But together, they were each
able to get something out

00:07:34.153 --> 00:07:35.354
of the trade.

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:37.056 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was more like
mutual prostitution.

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:40.460
And that was what led to
the birth of Woke Inc.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.762
It wasn't just woke and
it wasn't just the Inc,

00:07:42.762 --> 00:07:44.163
it was both of them.

00:07:44.163 --> 00:07:46.699
And that's what that
whole tale was about.

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:48.000 align:start size:84% position:16%
I will tell you
that over the course

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.502
of writing that book though,

00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:54.273
I was interested in both the
corporate cynicism behind it,

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:56.776
which is what that book
ended up being about.

00:07:56.776 --> 00:07:58.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I was also
interested in the content

00:07:58.044 --> 00:07:59.679
of the underlying
ideology as well.

00:07:59.679 --> 00:08:01.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so the publisher
kind of told me

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:03.416
and advisors who helped
me put that book together

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:05.351
and said, look, you gotta
pick one or the other.

00:08:05.351 --> 00:08:06.786
Start with the first one.

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:09.555 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then actually the
unfinished work was what led

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:10.790
to Nation of Victims,

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:12.492
which is the book that
I'm putting out now.

00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:14.494
So, you know, that was
a bit of the journey

00:08:14.494 --> 00:08:15.995
of the two books.

00:08:15.995 --> 00:08:19.732
The first one was really about
the corporate financial fuel

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:25.304
and the second book is really
about the underlying flyer,

00:08:25.304 --> 00:08:28.140
the fire, the configuration
on which corporations threw

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.609
that fuel.

00:08:29.609 --> 00:08:30.810 align:start size:94% position:6%
And together, I think
they describe the problem.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.112 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I assume that the
irony should not be lost

00:08:33.112 --> 00:08:34.814
on anyone that as
you describe it,

00:08:34.814 --> 00:08:38.618 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was capitalism that allowed
this thing to be unleashed,

00:08:38.618 --> 00:08:42.488
which is virulently
anti-capitalist.

00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:43.689
- Of course.

00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:45.124
And you know?

00:08:45.124 --> 00:08:48.961 align:start position:0%
Ludwig von Mises, a famous
economist, you know, Schumpeter,

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:51.430
Joseph Schumpeter
himself, you know,

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:52.865
said very similar things.

00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:56.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is early 20th century
stuff from von Mises

00:08:56.402 --> 00:09:01.340
that capitalism creates a
psychological need filled

00:09:02.808 --> 00:09:05.077
by anti-capitalism, you know.

00:09:05.077 --> 00:09:06.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
Schumpeter kind of
predicted the same thing,

00:09:06.746 --> 00:09:08.281 align:start size:84% position:16%
the system of
creative destruction.

00:09:08.281 --> 00:09:10.449
But first capitalism gives rise,

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:12.885 align:start size:84% position:16%
necessarily paves
the way to socialism.

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:14.754
That was kind of
Schumpeter thesis.

00:09:14.754 --> 00:09:16.322
And it's interesting.

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:18.324 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, we see
their predictions
play out in the moment

00:09:18.324 --> 00:09:19.659
that you and I live in.

00:09:19.659 --> 00:09:20.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, you're
technically a millennial.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.695
You're a millennial as well?

00:09:22.695 --> 00:09:25.031
- I believe I'm 46, my friend.

00:09:25.031 --> 00:09:26.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
I am Gen X.
- You're on the other side

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:27.266
of the bridge.

00:09:27.266 --> 00:09:28.401 align:start size:72% position:28%
- I am Gen X.
- Okay.

00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:29.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I am Gen X.
- You look good, man.

00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:30.002
- So, I don't know,

00:09:30.002 --> 00:09:30.803
but I'm a millennial.

00:09:30.803 --> 00:09:32.305
So I'm what, 37 now.

00:09:32.305 --> 00:09:34.440
So my generation, I was
gonna say our generation,

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:35.875
but we'll call it my generation.

00:09:35.875 --> 00:09:38.110
- I have a lot of
younger friends.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:39.579 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That's good, you know,
whatever you get it.

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:40.680
It's a spectrum anyway,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.281
Your generation sort of,

00:09:42.281 --> 00:09:44.850
the younger part of your
generation counts in this.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:45.785 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the millennials
in particular,

00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:47.253
we're on the receiving end.

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:50.022
I mean, I'm personally not
on the receiving end of this,

00:09:50.022 --> 00:09:52.658 align:start position:0%
but millennials as a generation
are on the receiving end

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:54.694 align:start position:0%
of the largest intergenerational
wealth transfer

00:09:54.694 --> 00:09:56.462
in human history.

00:09:56.462 --> 00:09:57.730
And there's something

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.165
about that that creates
this psychological need

00:09:59.165 --> 00:10:02.034 align:start size:97% position:3%
for self-criticism and
criticism of the very system

00:10:02.034 --> 00:10:04.136 align:start size:97% position:3%
that created the wealth that
you're otherwise gonna be

00:10:04.136 --> 00:10:05.404
on the receiving end of,

00:10:05.404 --> 00:10:07.073
but without actually
making the sacrifice

00:10:07.073 --> 00:10:08.507
of giving up that inheritance,

00:10:08.507 --> 00:10:10.343
to be able to apologize for it

00:10:10.343 --> 00:10:13.212
with this new self
found consciousness

00:10:13.212 --> 00:10:15.982
of your privilege or whatever
it is that lies at the heart

00:10:15.982 --> 00:10:18.618
of the apologism
that characterizes.

00:10:18.618 --> 00:10:20.119
And I don't even use
the word woke anymore,

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:21.988
'cause it's sort of
become a tired word,

00:10:21.988 --> 00:10:24.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the modern
Neo-progressive movement,

00:10:24.357 --> 00:10:26.025
the apologist culture,

00:10:26.025 --> 00:10:28.494
that's really at the
heart of my next book.

00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:30.830
Which is the victimhood culture

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:33.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
that permeates our
American fabric today.

00:10:33.833 --> 00:10:36.369
- Are you surprised
that it rampaged

00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:37.870
through so many businesses?

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:39.939 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean, businesses
that were were

00:10:39.939 --> 00:10:42.074
or should be based
on actual numbers.

00:10:42.074 --> 00:10:43.376
It's one thing, okay.

00:10:43.376 --> 00:10:45.945
It rampages through an
entertainment company,

00:10:45.945 --> 00:10:49.048 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, somebody making
movies, TV shows, et cetera,

00:10:49.048 --> 00:10:53.285 align:start size:97% position:3%
but like seemingly everything,
accounting firms and banks,

00:10:53.285 --> 00:10:55.054
like the whole freaking
thing, that, you know,

00:10:55.054 --> 00:10:56.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
these are people that actually
have to look at numbers

00:10:56.922 --> 00:11:00.926
and make sense based on reality
or at least are supposed to.

00:11:00.926 --> 00:11:03.729
- Yeah, you know, at
first it's surprising,

00:11:03.729 --> 00:11:05.398 align:start size:88% position:13%
of course it's
counterintuitive, right?

00:11:05.398 --> 00:11:07.099
That's why it made for
an interesting book.

00:11:07.099 --> 00:11:09.869
But when you examine it,

00:11:09.869 --> 00:11:10.803
it's actually not
surprising at all

00:11:10.803 --> 00:11:12.605
because it serves the interests

00:11:12.605 --> 00:11:16.542
of many of the people who
are the biggest propagators

00:11:16.542 --> 00:11:17.777
of this new ideology.

00:11:17.777 --> 00:11:20.046
I mean, look, Larry Fink
at BlackRock used this

00:11:20.046 --> 00:11:23.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
as a philosophy to
aggregate over $10 trillion,

00:11:23.282 --> 00:11:25.618 align:start size:88% position:13%
creating the world
assets under management.

00:11:25.618 --> 00:11:27.319
That's something that
he couldn't have done

00:11:27.319 --> 00:11:29.522 align:start size:88% position:13%
if it weren't fulfilling
the void created

00:11:29.522 --> 00:11:32.124 align:start size:94% position:6%
by the 2008 financial
crisis and telling people

00:11:32.124 --> 00:11:34.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
that this was actually
the new capitalism itself.

00:11:34.927 --> 00:11:37.029 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, you know, I
think the people on top,

00:11:37.029 --> 00:11:38.564 align:start size:81% position:19%
by the way without
saying an iota

00:11:38.564 --> 00:11:39.965
about those same words in China.

00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:41.600
How do you know it's
in their self-interest?

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.171
You talk about ESG or DEI or CSR

00:11:45.171 --> 00:11:47.640 align:start position:0%
or whatever your three favorite
three letter acronym is,

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.508
when they go over to the
other three letter acronym

00:11:49.508 --> 00:11:52.845
from ESG, DEI and CSR to CCP,

00:11:52.845 --> 00:11:54.847
to be able to do business in
China without saying a peak

00:11:54.847 --> 00:11:57.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
about ESG or DEI, because
that's what it pays

00:11:57.416 --> 00:11:59.085
to do over there.

00:11:59.085 --> 00:12:00.519
Now in the United States,

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:01.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
it paid to do it because
pension funds like California,

00:12:01.787 --> 00:12:04.356
CalPERS, you know, state
of New York, they said,

00:12:04.356 --> 00:12:06.058
we're only gonna do
business with firms

00:12:06.058 --> 00:12:08.961 align:start size:88% position:13%
that adopt these
progressive commitments.

00:12:08.961 --> 00:12:10.429
Well, they said, you know what?

00:12:10.429 --> 00:12:12.431 align:start size:97% position:3%
Those are where the biggest
checks are coming from.

00:12:12.431 --> 00:12:14.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's the tune
we're gonna dance to.

00:12:14.433 --> 00:12:16.001
And so in a certain sense,

00:12:16.001 --> 00:12:18.003 align:start size:97% position:3%
when the economic incentives
were set up that way,

00:12:18.003 --> 00:12:20.406 align:start position:0%
it wasn't altogether surprising
that people responded

00:12:20.406 --> 00:12:22.007
to those incentives.

00:12:22.007 --> 00:12:24.376
I think, you know, my new
book is Nation of Victims.

00:12:24.376 --> 00:12:27.580
We sort of talk about that at
the individual level as well.

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:30.015
Why is victimhood culture so
rampant in the United States?

00:12:30.015 --> 00:12:31.751
It's a bit of a
curiosity, right?

00:12:31.751 --> 00:12:34.086
You know, we have living in
the most prosperous moments

00:12:34.086 --> 00:12:35.788
in human history and
in American history,

00:12:35.788 --> 00:12:39.325 align:start size:94% position:6%
why is it then that we
fashion ourselves to think

00:12:39.325 --> 00:12:42.128
of ourselves as victims
in every given direction?

00:12:42.128 --> 00:12:44.029
Black victims, white victims,

00:12:44.029 --> 00:12:45.331
second generation Asian victims.

00:12:45.331 --> 00:12:46.766
I mean, it's everywhere.

00:12:46.766 --> 00:12:48.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
We in certain senses create
an incentive structure

00:12:48.968 --> 00:12:50.402 align:start size:84% position:16%
in our country that
rewards someone

00:12:50.402 --> 00:12:52.404 align:start size:84% position:16%
for characterizing
themselves as a victim

00:12:52.404 --> 00:12:53.806
rather than as a victor.

00:12:53.806 --> 00:12:56.275
And so I think that, you know,

00:12:56.275 --> 00:12:58.377
it's not a sort of a
cheesy humans respond

00:12:58.377 --> 00:13:00.312 align:start size:94% position:6%
to incentives kind of
argument all the way down,

00:13:00.312 --> 00:13:02.548 align:start size:88% position:13%
but that is an important
part of the story.

00:13:02.548 --> 00:13:04.049
And it's certainly
a part of the story

00:13:04.049 --> 00:13:06.318
that makes it less surprising
than you'd otherwise think.

00:13:06.318 --> 00:13:09.288
- So I wanna get to some of
that psychology behind that.

00:13:09.288 --> 00:13:10.790
Real quick, just to back up.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:12.625
I think most of my audience
is following you on BlackRock

00:13:12.625 --> 00:13:13.859
and ESG and some
of the other stuff,

00:13:13.859 --> 00:13:15.628 align:start size:88% position:13%
but for some of the
people that are missing,

00:13:15.628 --> 00:13:17.329
maybe some of the pieces here.

00:13:17.329 --> 00:13:21.967
First off, can you explain sort
of what BlackRock is and how

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:23.335
that is connected to ESG?

00:13:23.335 --> 00:13:25.204 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've talked a bit
about ESG on the show,

00:13:25.204 --> 00:13:27.540
but I think if we kind of
lay it out somewhat simply,

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.842
I think that'll
help some people.

00:13:29.842 --> 00:13:31.110
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.479
So look, you know,
the ESG movement,

00:13:33.479 --> 00:13:34.413
people hear this acronym.

00:13:34.413 --> 00:13:35.314
I mean, what is it, okay?

00:13:35.314 --> 00:13:36.816
So it stands

00:13:36.816 --> 00:13:39.285
for Environmental, Social
and Governance factors.

00:13:39.285 --> 00:13:41.120
And it basically
is a close cousin

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:44.590 align:start size:97% position:3%
of a broader movement called
stakeholder capitalism,

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:47.326
which refers to the idea
that businesses should not

00:13:47.326 --> 00:13:50.429
just sell products and
services for profit,

00:13:50.429 --> 00:13:54.233 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they should also advance
other societal agendas

00:13:54.233 --> 00:13:56.669
and other social
interests, okay.

00:13:56.669 --> 00:13:57.870
That's the philosophy.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:13:59.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
Agree or not,
that's what it says.

00:13:59.405 --> 00:14:04.009
Now, what that's become is a
vehicle for government actors

00:14:05.144 --> 00:14:06.912
to effectuate through
the private sector

00:14:06.912 --> 00:14:10.316 align:start size:97% position:3%
what they could not effectuate
through the front door

00:14:10.316 --> 00:14:11.784
of policy making.

00:14:11.784 --> 00:14:14.987
So one example let's take
is the climate agenda,

00:14:14.987 --> 00:14:17.489 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Green New Deal, whatever
you may want to call it.

00:14:17.489 --> 00:14:19.291
The Green New Deal did not
muster political support

00:14:19.291 --> 00:14:20.793
to be passed through Congress.

00:14:20.793 --> 00:14:23.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, what the ESG movement
offered was a vehicle

00:14:23.229 --> 00:14:24.597
to lean on many companies

00:14:24.597 --> 00:14:27.566
to sign what you call a
climate pledge instead,

00:14:27.566 --> 00:14:30.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
get every company to
voluntarily, "voluntarily",

00:14:30.302 --> 00:14:31.537
by the way.
(Dave laughs)

00:14:31.537 --> 00:14:32.805
I use that in air
quotes because it's

00:14:32.805 --> 00:14:34.206
with the invisible fist
of government behind it,

00:14:34.206 --> 00:14:38.444 align:start size:88% position:13%
but to voluntarily
adopt net zero standards

00:14:38.444 --> 00:14:40.012
and pledges by 2050,

00:14:40.012 --> 00:14:42.848 align:start size:97% position:3%
unilateral pledges to support
the Paris Climate Accords

00:14:42.848 --> 00:14:44.450
and abide by its demands,

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:47.553 align:start size:84% position:16%
even though the US
did not ratify the
Paris Climate Accords,

00:14:47.553 --> 00:14:49.221
the Paris Climate Agreement.

00:14:49.221 --> 00:14:51.757 align:start size:97% position:3%
The private sector effectively
ratifies it instead.

00:14:51.757 --> 00:14:53.525
I actually, this an aside,

00:14:53.525 --> 00:14:55.327 align:start size:84% position:16%
I sent a public letter
as a shareholder

00:14:55.327 --> 00:14:58.797 align:start size:97% position:3%
to the board of Chevron
earlier this week complaining

00:14:58.797 --> 00:15:00.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
amongst other things
about a corporate behavior

00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:02.668
about Chevron embracing the
Paris Climate Agreement,

00:15:02.668 --> 00:15:04.203
reminding them that the
US did not ratify this.

00:15:04.203 --> 00:15:06.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is not Chevron's job
to use shareholder capital

00:15:06.906 --> 00:15:08.407
to ratify it instead,

00:15:08.407 --> 00:15:10.242
especially when it's not
even in the best interests

00:15:10.242 --> 00:15:11.443
of that business.

00:15:11.443 --> 00:15:13.779 align:start size:88% position:13%
So anyway, that's a
bit about what this is.

00:15:13.779 --> 00:15:15.814
You know, let's just say even
the Silicon Valley censorship.

00:15:15.814 --> 00:15:18.083 align:start size:97% position:3%
I actually view that as a
subset of this broader problem

00:15:18.083 --> 00:15:20.619 align:start size:94% position:6%
of stakeholder capitalism
and the ESG movement.

00:15:20.619 --> 00:15:22.321
People think about that
as a separate issue.

00:15:22.321 --> 00:15:25.624 align:start position:0%
I've written extensively about,
you know, about that issue,

00:15:27.826 --> 00:15:29.094
about how private actors
actually ought to be bound

00:15:29.094 --> 00:15:31.163
by the First Amendment
if they're responding

00:15:31.163 --> 00:15:32.398
to government threats.

00:15:32.398 --> 00:15:33.432
I, you know, was
talking about that

00:15:33.432 --> 00:15:34.700 align:start size:84% position:16%
before that was cool.
- Yeah.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:35.834
- Back when it was ridiculed

00:15:35.834 --> 00:15:37.169
as an idea about a year and ago.

00:15:37.169 --> 00:15:39.171 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It's sort of like when
Jen Saki is admitting

00:15:39.171 --> 00:15:41.607
that the government flags posts

00:15:41.607 --> 00:15:44.310 align:start size:88% position:13%
about disinformation
for Facebook in essence.

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.211 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Absolutely, and then
threatens companies

00:15:46.211 --> 00:15:47.413 align:start size:81% position:19%
if they don't take
down the post,

00:15:47.413 --> 00:15:49.748
that they're gonna be broken up,

00:15:49.748 --> 00:15:51.483 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they're gonna
bring antitrust action,

00:15:51.483 --> 00:15:52.818
then companies do
it, but they say,

00:15:52.818 --> 00:15:53.886 align:start size:84% position:16%
hey, it's not the
government doing it.

00:15:53.886 --> 00:15:55.321
It's just the free market.

00:15:55.321 --> 00:15:57.289 align:start position:0%
And the First Amendment doesn't
apply to the free market.

00:15:57.289 --> 00:15:58.490
It's another example

00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:01.093
of government actors
using private companies

00:16:01.093 --> 00:16:03.696
to do through the back door
the dirty work of government

00:16:03.696 --> 00:16:05.464 align:start size:97% position:3%
that it could not accomplish
through the front door

00:16:05.464 --> 00:16:08.434 align:start size:97% position:3%
under the constitution or
with political accountability.

00:16:08.434 --> 00:16:11.570 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that's what this movement
effectively represents.

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:15.240
Now, it, you know, sort of in
a takes two to tango spirit.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.811 align:start size:97% position:3%
It only works if the consumers
and individual investors,

00:16:18.811 --> 00:16:20.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
et cetera, in the economy
still fall for it.

00:16:20.679 --> 00:16:22.014
At the end of the day,

00:16:22.014 --> 00:16:22.948
if consumers would
still resist this

00:16:22.948 --> 00:16:24.216
and not buy those products

00:16:24.216 --> 00:16:26.685
or individual investors
who exercise their voice

00:16:26.685 --> 00:16:28.387 align:start size:88% position:13%
not gonna stand by this
behavior, you know,

00:16:28.387 --> 00:16:29.688
this couldn't have existed,

00:16:29.688 --> 00:16:31.090
but I think some of that relates

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:33.392 align:start size:81% position:19%
to a moment of
cultural weakness.

00:16:33.392 --> 00:16:35.995 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so I think there's
not one story here,

00:16:35.995 --> 00:16:38.097
but two storylines that
are really important.

00:16:38.097 --> 00:16:40.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
One is the top down
problem, where, you know,

00:16:40.799 --> 00:16:42.001
actors like BlackRock,

00:16:42.001 --> 00:16:43.569 align:start size:84% position:16%
the world's largest
asset manager.

00:16:43.569 --> 00:16:45.004
You asked who they are,

00:16:45.004 --> 00:16:46.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're the world's largest
asset manager managing

00:16:46.205 --> 00:16:48.040 align:start size:88% position:13%
over $10 trillion of
everyday citizens money.

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
What they do is they
manage that money

00:16:49.541 --> 00:16:51.610 align:start size:84% position:16%
by investing in
American companies,

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:53.846
but then to show up in the
boardrooms of those companies

00:16:53.846 --> 00:16:57.316
and say that, you know what,
we're not gonna promote you

00:16:58.917 --> 00:17:00.786
or we're not gonna pay you,

00:17:00.786 --> 00:17:02.421
or we're gonna vote
your directors off

00:17:02.421 --> 00:17:06.325 align:start size:97% position:3%
unless you adopt these
environmental social agendas

00:17:06.325 --> 00:17:07.826
that we demand.

00:17:07.826 --> 00:17:11.063 align:start size:97% position:3%
But they're using the money
of everyday citizens to do it.

00:17:11.063 --> 00:17:13.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
Most of those everyday
citizens actually disagree

00:17:13.132 --> 00:17:14.066
with that agenda.

00:17:14.066 --> 00:17:15.934
So that's the top down problem.

00:17:15.934 --> 00:17:19.071 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's a big breach of fiduciary
duty using $10 trillion

00:17:19.071 --> 00:17:21.507
of someone else's money to
advance political agendas

00:17:21.507 --> 00:17:23.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the everyday citizen,
the owner of capital,

00:17:23.042 --> 00:17:23.976
disagrees with.

00:17:23.976 --> 00:17:25.411
That's the top down problem.

00:17:25.411 --> 00:17:26.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the bottom up problem
is we have a culture

00:17:26.945 --> 00:17:29.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's also falling for it and
saying that, you know what?

00:17:29.448 --> 00:17:32.351
We actually do demand that
companies behave in ways

00:17:32.351 --> 00:17:35.988 align:start size:88% position:13%
that align with our
Neo-progressive values.

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:38.090
And then that gives those
boardrooms cover to be able

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:39.858
to say, well, this is what
consumers are demanding,

00:17:39.858 --> 00:17:42.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's what allows Nike
or Disney to behave in the way

00:17:42.061 --> 00:17:43.362
that it does.

00:17:43.362 --> 00:17:44.596
So it's complicated.

00:17:44.596 --> 00:17:46.298 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so the first book
was basically Woke Inc.,

00:17:46.298 --> 00:17:48.901
was about the top down problem.

00:17:48.901 --> 00:17:50.769
The problem of the
woke capitalist,

00:17:50.769 --> 00:17:52.971 align:start size:84% position:16%
the use and abuse,
the use, I should say,

00:17:52.971 --> 00:17:55.774
the abuse of capital to be able

00:17:55.774 --> 00:17:59.778
to effectuate these political
agendas through the back door

00:17:59.778 --> 00:18:01.747 align:start size:84% position:16%
that government
couldn't effectuate
through the front door

00:18:01.747 --> 00:18:04.083
and that cynical
arranged marriage.

00:18:04.083 --> 00:18:06.185
But the second half of
it is this other problem.

00:18:06.185 --> 00:18:08.487
The generational
cultural problem.

00:18:08.487 --> 00:18:09.721
The victimhood cancer,

00:18:09.721 --> 00:18:11.156
that's the heart of
Nation of Victims,

00:18:11.156 --> 00:18:15.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
the next book that explains
this cultural deficit

00:18:16.595 --> 00:18:19.531
that allows companies
and government actors

00:18:19.531 --> 00:18:22.868 align:start size:88% position:13%
to cynically prey on our
cultural insecurities

00:18:22.868 --> 00:18:24.369
as a generation.

00:18:24.369 --> 00:18:25.604
And I think that's equally an
important part of the story.

00:18:25.604 --> 00:18:27.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Okay, so we'll get
to that in a sec,

00:18:27.005 --> 00:18:29.374 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause I think that sort
of the spiritual part

00:18:29.374 --> 00:18:31.443 align:start size:97% position:3%
of this that I think many
people are missing right now.

00:18:31.443 --> 00:18:33.712
They keep thinking the answer
to this problem is some sort

00:18:33.712 --> 00:18:35.614
of government answer
and it's like, no,

00:18:35.614 --> 00:18:37.483
that's kind of how we got here.

00:18:37.483 --> 00:18:39.284
But just to jump
back for a second.

00:18:39.284 --> 00:18:41.987 align:start size:88% position:13%
You mentioned the
fiduciary responsibility

00:18:41.987 --> 00:18:44.556
that these boards have
to their shareholders.

00:18:44.556 --> 00:18:48.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
Are you surprised or was
there an example of a company

00:18:48.026 --> 00:18:50.929 align:start position:0%
where the shareholders were
able to fight this effectively?

00:18:50.929 --> 00:18:53.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I remember
literally on my show five

00:18:53.132 --> 00:18:56.268 align:start size:97% position:3%
or six years ago when Google
AdSense was clamping down

00:18:56.268 --> 00:18:57.803
on everybody.

00:18:57.803 --> 00:18:59.505
I had a couple guests in a row
and I was calling for it too.

00:18:59.505 --> 00:19:01.673
It's like, where are the
shareholders who are going,

00:19:01.673 --> 00:19:04.176 align:start size:97% position:3%
well, wait a minute, put
censorship aside for a second.

00:19:04.176 --> 00:19:06.578
If they are not putting
ads on certain things,

00:19:06.578 --> 00:19:09.114
am I being hurt
as a shareholder?

00:19:10.516 --> 00:19:11.950 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, are there any examples
where the shareholders

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:15.387 align:start size:97% position:3%
actually accomplished anything
or lawsuits or anything?

00:19:15.387 --> 00:19:18.924
- Not a ton yet. That's what
I'm working on, man. (laughs)

00:19:18.924 --> 00:19:19.758
- (laughs) Right. All right.

00:19:19.758 --> 00:19:21.260
- I started Strive.

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:23.128
So Strive is a new firm
competing with BlackRock.

00:19:23.128 --> 00:19:25.564
We just sent a public letter
to Chevron calling out a lot

00:19:25.564 --> 00:19:29.001 align:start size:88% position:13%
of these behaviors
that, crucially though,

00:19:29.001 --> 00:19:31.136
were forced on them by
BlackRock, State Street,

00:19:31.136 --> 00:19:32.371
and Vanguard.

00:19:32.371 --> 00:19:35.340
In many cases, Chevron
actually said initially

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:37.776
that they opposed
these proposals,

00:19:37.776 --> 00:19:41.013 align:start position:0%
but then the large shareholders
vote for them anyway.

00:19:41.013 --> 00:19:42.514
And this is the problem, Drew,

00:19:43.916 --> 00:19:47.753
a lot of the movement,
you know, that, you know,

00:19:47.753 --> 00:19:50.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
you would advocate for
shareholder-centric power.

00:19:50.355 --> 00:19:53.692
What they miss is that
the 21st century version

00:19:53.692 --> 00:19:55.160
of this problem is different

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:58.564 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they've thrown a trip wire
that a lot of us fell for,

00:19:58.564 --> 00:20:00.732
which was that they said,
okay, Milton Friedman,

00:20:00.732 --> 00:20:02.734 align:start size:94% position:6%
you said that
shareholders rule, right?

00:20:02.734 --> 00:20:04.636 align:start size:97% position:3%
And company management teams
report to shareholders.

00:20:04.636 --> 00:20:06.572
Well, guess what?

00:20:06.572 --> 00:20:08.640
We are the shareholder.

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:10.108
And we are demanding

00:20:10.108 --> 00:20:13.078 align:start position:0%
that you the company management
adopt these social agendas.

00:20:13.078 --> 00:20:16.014 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're not betraying your
fiduciary duty to us.

00:20:16.014 --> 00:20:18.750
You're actually working for us
'cause we're the shareholder.

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:21.286
Milton Friedman all
along said it, rule,

00:20:21.286 --> 00:20:22.487
and you owe the duty to us

00:20:22.487 --> 00:20:23.689 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we the shareholder
are demanding it.

00:20:23.689 --> 00:20:25.624 align:start size:84% position:16%
So here's the fallacy
in that argument.

00:20:25.624 --> 00:20:28.393 align:start size:97% position:3%
But that's effectively what
allowed this problem to spread

00:20:28.393 --> 00:20:31.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
like wildfire is the
parlance of fiduciary duty

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:34.700
to shareholder misses the
fact that the shareholder,

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.001
I use shareholder air quotes,

00:20:36.001 --> 00:20:37.669
is actually the one
pushing the agenda.

00:20:37.669 --> 00:20:39.671
So to do this, we have
to get a little academic

00:20:39.671 --> 00:20:40.839
for a second.

00:20:40.839 --> 00:20:41.773
- Let's roll. Let's roll.

00:20:41.773 --> 00:20:43.242
I got a bright audience.

00:20:43.242 --> 00:20:44.076 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It'll be all right.
- If you don't follow this,

00:20:44.076 --> 00:20:45.377
I'll stop, but okay.

00:20:46.812 --> 00:20:50.482
So the whole shareholder
privacy doctrine came from,

00:20:50.482 --> 00:20:53.785 align:start size:88% position:13%
it was a vestige of 20th
century corporate law

00:20:53.785 --> 00:20:56.722 align:start size:97% position:3%
and even Friedman, I thinking
in the late 20th century,

00:20:56.722 --> 00:21:00.259
that solved a problem called
the separation of ownership

00:21:00.259 --> 00:21:02.227
and control, okay.

00:21:02.227 --> 00:21:04.062 align:start size:84% position:16%
So the CEO has
control of the firm,

00:21:04.062 --> 00:21:06.064 align:start size:84% position:16%
but the shareholder
owns the firm.

00:21:06.064 --> 00:21:07.532
They're not the same person,

00:21:07.532 --> 00:21:11.136 align:start position:0%
that gives rise to what's called
a principle agent conflict.

00:21:11.136 --> 00:21:13.005
The shareholders, the
principle, the CEOs,

00:21:13.005 --> 00:21:15.774 align:start size:84% position:16%
the agent leads to the
private jet problem

00:21:15.774 --> 00:21:18.510 align:start size:94% position:6%
of, you know, CEOs that
are flying on private jets

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:20.912 align:start size:84% position:16%
using the shareholder
capital to do it.

00:21:20.912 --> 00:21:23.248 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, that RJR Nabisco
problem, over spending.

00:21:23.248 --> 00:21:24.950
Well, how do you close that gap?

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:27.653
It's by imposing
a fiduciary duty

00:21:27.653 --> 00:21:31.356
on the CEO to the shareholder.

00:21:31.356 --> 00:21:32.924
So Milton Friedman said the CEO

00:21:32.924 --> 00:21:34.326 align:start size:81% position:19%
can't pursue these
social agendas

00:21:34.326 --> 00:21:36.561 align:start size:97% position:3%
without serving the
interest of the shareholder.

00:21:36.561 --> 00:21:38.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's where the
fiduciary duty came from.

00:21:38.397 --> 00:21:39.931
That's the 20th century stuff.

00:21:39.931 --> 00:21:42.134
It's called the separation
of ownership and control.

00:21:43.602 --> 00:21:47.272
The 21st century version of
this problem is different.

00:21:47.272 --> 00:21:51.009
It is not the separation
of ownership and control.

00:21:51.009 --> 00:21:55.113
It is the separation of
ownership and ownership.

00:21:55.113 --> 00:21:58.050 align:start size:97% position:3%
And what I mean by that is
the parties who now then claim

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.552 align:start position:0%
to be the owners, the
shareholders of these companies

00:22:00.552 --> 00:22:02.788
like BlackRock, State
Street, and Vanguard,

00:22:02.788 --> 00:22:06.591
they are not the actual
owners of those companies

00:22:06.591 --> 00:22:09.361
because they launched these
so-called passive index funds

00:22:09.361 --> 00:22:12.764
that aggregated trillions of
dollars in the last 15 years

00:22:13.932 --> 00:22:15.634
that are the money
of everyday citizens

00:22:15.634 --> 00:22:17.035 align:start size:84% position:16%
who are the actual
owners of the company,

00:22:17.035 --> 00:22:21.273 align:start size:94% position:6%
but invested via vehicles
that were managed

00:22:21.273 --> 00:22:24.109 align:start position:0%
by a managerial clique that
then showed up in the boardrooms

00:22:24.109 --> 00:22:26.345
of those companies and
said, you know what?

00:22:26.345 --> 00:22:27.813
I am the shareholder and I'm
demanding you behave this way.

00:22:27.813 --> 00:22:29.047
But the class-

00:22:29.047 --> 00:22:30.549
- So basically in
essence though,

00:22:30.549 --> 00:22:31.383
the average shareholder
probably didn't even know

00:22:31.383 --> 00:22:32.617
what was going on.

00:22:32.617 --> 00:22:33.618
- They didn't know
what's going on.

00:22:33.618 --> 00:22:35.120
They didn't know
what's going on.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:36.355
So the way I talk about this
is no longer as shareholders,

00:22:36.355 --> 00:22:37.789
but as the owners
of the company,

00:22:37.789 --> 00:22:40.726 align:start size:97% position:3%
the actual end owner of
capital, the everyday citizen,

00:22:40.726 --> 00:22:43.195
whose money is being invested

00:22:43.195 --> 00:22:45.997
through a plinko chips
mediated financial system

00:22:45.997 --> 00:22:47.933 align:start size:84% position:16%
that then goes into
a pension fund,

00:22:47.933 --> 00:22:50.268
that then asks an investment
consultant where to invest,

00:22:50.268 --> 00:22:52.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
that then invests in a
fund managed by BlackRock,

00:22:52.371 --> 00:22:54.706
that then invests in a company.

00:22:54.706 --> 00:22:56.375 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's such an
intermediated system,

00:22:56.375 --> 00:22:58.944
but the actual owner
of capital has no idea

00:22:58.944 --> 00:23:00.912
that that state pension
fund and its board member

00:23:00.912 --> 00:23:02.314
and its investment staff

00:23:02.314 --> 00:23:05.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
and BlackRock were
betraying their interests

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:06.585
by representing viewpoints

00:23:06.585 --> 00:23:07.953 align:start size:84% position:16%
in corporate
America's boardrooms,

00:23:07.953 --> 00:23:09.321
in the boardrooms of
companies like Chevron,

00:23:09.321 --> 00:23:11.890
the one I wrote to
earlier this week,

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:13.125
in ways that the owner

00:23:13.125 --> 00:23:15.460
of capital had no idea
was actually happening.

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:18.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
So that's really how
this plays out in reality

00:23:18.296 --> 00:23:20.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
where the parties who claim
to be the shareholders

00:23:20.932 --> 00:23:25.003 align:start position:0%
are not the actual shareholders
and that's the big problem.

00:23:25.003 --> 00:23:26.505
- Okay, so with that in mind,

00:23:26.505 --> 00:23:28.640
let's shift a little bit to
the sort of spiritual part

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:30.108
of this or the generational,

00:23:30.108 --> 00:23:32.377
the cultural part that
you're talking about.

00:23:32.377 --> 00:23:35.180
You mentioned before
from Indian background,

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:36.615
you grew up in the Midwest.

00:23:36.615 --> 00:23:39.017 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, I suspect that
whether it was your parents

00:23:39.017 --> 00:23:41.319 align:start size:97% position:3%
or great grandparents or
whatever it might be that came

00:23:41.319 --> 00:23:43.822
from India, I'm guessing
they didn't come with a lot

00:23:43.822 --> 00:23:45.323
and they probably had to work.

00:23:45.323 --> 00:23:46.792
- They didn't come with a
lot. Nope, they did not.

00:23:46.792 --> 00:23:48.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And I'm guessing that
they don't feel very guilty

00:23:48.093 --> 00:23:49.728
about your success.

00:23:49.728 --> 00:23:51.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
- No, they don't,
and that's the thing.

00:23:51.696 --> 00:23:53.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
Whether they're
Republican, Democrat,

00:23:53.398 --> 00:23:55.200
the thing you find
about immigrants who
come to this country

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.836
through the front door,
and I distinguish that,

00:23:57.836 --> 00:23:59.104 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, for coming
through the back.

00:23:59.104 --> 00:24:00.705
Legal immigrants
by and large come

00:24:00.705 --> 00:24:02.841
to this country to
pursue excellence,

00:24:02.841 --> 00:24:05.143
to pursue their dreams,
to pursue excellence,

00:24:05.143 --> 00:24:07.412
without having to
apologize for it.

00:24:07.412 --> 00:24:09.881 align:start size:84% position:16%
Leaving places that
were anti-meritocratic

00:24:09.881 --> 00:24:12.617
to come to a meritocratic
nation where regardless

00:24:12.617 --> 00:24:14.352
of your creed or skin color,

00:24:14.352 --> 00:24:16.621
you can achieve anything you
want with your own hard work

00:24:16.621 --> 00:24:17.923
and commitment and dedication.

00:24:17.923 --> 00:24:20.525
That used to be our
national identity.

00:24:20.525 --> 00:24:22.794 align:start size:88% position:13%
We now have a new
national identity built

00:24:22.794 --> 00:24:24.196
on victimhood instead.

00:24:24.196 --> 00:24:25.464
And one of the things, you know,

00:24:25.464 --> 00:24:27.599
my parents taught me,
say from a young age,

00:24:27.599 --> 00:24:30.335
is that hardship is
not the same thing

00:24:30.335 --> 00:24:32.571
as you're gonna encounter
hardship in your life.

00:24:32.571 --> 00:24:34.706 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's part of what
teaches you who you are.

00:24:36.141 --> 00:24:39.411
But we live now in a moment
where we kids are taught,

00:24:39.411 --> 00:24:41.513
the next generation,
by the way, you know,

00:24:41.513 --> 00:24:42.614
I was first generation, my
parents were the immigrants.

00:24:42.614 --> 00:24:43.548
I was born in America.

00:24:43.548 --> 00:24:44.749
The second and third generation,

00:24:44.749 --> 00:24:46.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
even in the Asian
American communities,

00:24:46.518 --> 00:24:48.086
are being taught this new
narrative to say that,

00:24:48.086 --> 00:24:49.354
you know what?

00:24:49.354 --> 00:24:50.889
The more you think of
yourself as a victim,

00:24:50.889 --> 00:24:52.390
as a person of color,

00:24:52.390 --> 00:24:54.059 align:start size:84% position:16%
or whatever your
victim narrative is,

00:24:54.059 --> 00:24:55.727 align:start size:81% position:19%
that's how you
get ahead in life,

00:24:55.727 --> 00:24:58.563 align:start position:0%
not by actually unapologetically
embracing the pursuit

00:24:58.563 --> 00:24:59.831
of excellence.

00:24:59.831 --> 00:25:01.299
And that's become our
new national identity.

00:25:01.299 --> 00:25:03.735
A national idea that was
built on the shared pursuit

00:25:03.735 --> 00:25:05.570
of excellence has
since perverted

00:25:05.570 --> 00:25:08.573 align:start position:0%
into this new national identity
that that's taken the form

00:25:08.573 --> 00:25:10.976
of this victimhood
grievance Olympics

00:25:10.976 --> 00:25:12.677
where there is no gold medalist.

00:25:12.677 --> 00:25:14.546
It is our nation and
our national identity

00:25:14.546 --> 00:25:16.047
that loses in the end.

00:25:16.047 --> 00:25:18.116 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's the heart of this
whole new book, you know.

00:25:18.116 --> 00:25:20.619 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's why I wrote the
book is someone who needed

00:25:20.619 --> 00:25:23.154 align:start size:97% position:3%
to expose this in some ways
as a first generation American

00:25:23.154 --> 00:25:25.190 align:start position:0%
looking at this, not as
somebody who inherited America,

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.425
but whose parents came to
this country voluntarily

00:25:27.425 --> 00:25:30.862 align:start size:97% position:3%
with their two feet to be part
of that national identity.

00:25:30.862 --> 00:25:32.998
And yet now, 40 years later,

00:25:32.998 --> 00:25:35.367 align:start size:84% position:16%
found a new national
identity centered

00:25:35.367 --> 00:25:37.869
on this black hole of
victimhood instead.

00:25:39.304 --> 00:25:42.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Did the Neo-libs or the
libs or the Neo-progressives

00:25:42.474 --> 00:25:43.742
or whatever version,

00:25:43.742 --> 00:25:45.277
whatever language you
wanna use around that,

00:25:45.277 --> 00:25:49.014
is there some reason that they
inherently failed with this?

00:25:49.014 --> 00:25:51.816 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, that they
didn't defend the ideas

00:25:51.816 --> 00:25:55.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
that their ancestors came
here for well enough?

00:25:55.287 --> 00:25:56.388
- Yes, so I think some

00:25:56.388 --> 00:25:57.956 align:start size:88% position:13%
of it was this
generational insecurity.

00:25:57.956 --> 00:25:59.524
I think it's this idea

00:25:59.524 --> 00:26:03.128 align:start position:0%
that when you haven't
encountered hardship, actually,

00:26:03.128 --> 00:26:06.064
you begin to hunger
for it. (chuckles)

00:26:06.064 --> 00:26:07.165 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, there's something
about the human psyche.

00:26:07.165 --> 00:26:08.767
It's a funny thing.

00:26:08.767 --> 00:26:13.171
We require hardship and the
experience of overcoming it

00:26:13.171 --> 00:26:16.675 align:start position:0%
to remind ourselves the contours
of our soul, of who we are.

00:26:16.675 --> 00:26:19.611
I gave the high school
commencement address

00:26:19.611 --> 00:26:22.213
to my own high school
where I graduated.

00:26:22.213 --> 00:26:24.282 align:start position:0%
I gave commencement address
when, I graduated what, in 2003.

00:26:24.282 --> 00:26:27.586
They invited me back to give
an updated address last year

00:26:27.586 --> 00:26:29.354
at last year's commencement.

00:26:29.354 --> 00:26:30.589
And it struck me,

00:26:30.589 --> 00:26:32.090
that was the first
graduating class

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:34.392 align:start size:97% position:3%
that had in-person graduation
were not a single member

00:26:34.392 --> 00:26:37.896 align:start size:84% position:16%
of that class was
even born on the day

00:26:37.896 --> 00:26:40.665
that those twin towers were
hit by those two planes.

00:26:40.665 --> 00:26:42.834
And that defined part of
my high school experience.

00:26:42.834 --> 00:26:44.269
I mean, I was in high school,

00:26:44.269 --> 00:26:45.604 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was in AP history
class or US history class

00:26:45.604 --> 00:26:46.838
at that particular time.

00:26:46.838 --> 00:26:50.408
It was, you know, it
was a defining moment.

00:26:50.408 --> 00:26:51.943 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is you're looking
at a generation

00:26:51.943 --> 00:26:54.846
where not a single person had
been through that experience.

00:26:54.846 --> 00:26:56.147 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I'm not saying
that it had to be 9/11,

00:26:56.147 --> 00:26:59.651
but, you know, I think a
generation that actually

00:26:59.651 --> 00:27:01.019
is on the receiving end

00:27:01.019 --> 00:27:02.654 align:start size:94% position:6%
of that intergenerational
wealth transfer

00:27:02.654 --> 00:27:07.325 align:start size:97% position:3%
that hasn't had to necessarily
as either as a citizen

00:27:07.325 --> 00:27:12.163
or as an individual fight for
the kind of right to be able

00:27:12.163 --> 00:27:16.167
to be proud, needs to then
see themselves as the victim

00:27:16.167 --> 00:27:18.203
of some other hardship instead.

00:27:18.203 --> 00:27:20.572
And so you could argue, maybe
it's not even their fault.

00:27:20.572 --> 00:27:21.973
This is just what the arc

00:27:21.973 --> 00:27:24.709 align:start position:0%
of history has dealt our
generation, millennials, Gen Z,

00:27:24.709 --> 00:27:26.978
and younger, to have
to think of themselves

00:27:26.978 --> 00:27:29.681 align:start size:97% position:3%
as encountering hardships that
don't even actually exist,

00:27:29.681 --> 00:27:33.084 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then reinvent that
victimhood narrative
until a new one

00:27:33.084 --> 00:27:35.420
is dealt in its place instead.

00:27:35.420 --> 00:27:37.789
But the problem is, if you live
in that victimhood narrative

00:27:37.789 --> 00:27:40.191
for long enough, you're not
even gonna have the fortitude

00:27:40.191 --> 00:27:41.860
to fight that hardship
when it actually comes.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:43.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe that's China, maybe
it's something else.

00:27:43.728 --> 00:27:46.798
Whatever it is, if we don't
turn this culture around,

00:27:46.798 --> 00:27:49.868 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think we will have actually
lost the very fortitude

00:27:49.868 --> 00:27:52.270
that we might have gotten
from actually encountering

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:53.972
more part of the
diagnosis for me.

00:27:55.373 --> 00:27:56.741
- Yeah, it's interesting
you're making a distinction

00:27:56.741 --> 00:27:58.843 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I try to bring
up all the time

00:27:58.843 --> 00:28:00.045
that it's perceived oppression,

00:28:00.045 --> 00:28:02.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
not necessarily even
real oppression.

00:28:02.047 --> 00:28:03.281
- That's right.

00:28:03.281 --> 00:28:04.549
- It's something that's
made up in their mind.

00:28:04.549 --> 00:28:06.551
So where do you think
big tech fits in this?

00:28:06.551 --> 00:28:08.887
You know, when we hear
things like, well, okay,

00:28:08.887 --> 00:28:11.956
all of this gender stuff
and all of this woke stuff,

00:28:11.956 --> 00:28:15.226
it's pushed on all of kids
in America through TikTok,

00:28:15.226 --> 00:28:16.695
but it's banned in China.

00:28:16.695 --> 00:28:19.230 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you think that there
might be a connection there?

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:21.366
- Yeah, or even the parent
company that owns TikTok

00:28:21.366 --> 00:28:23.535 align:start size:97% position:3%
in China actually uses it
to like educate kids on math

00:28:23.535 --> 00:28:25.003
and science and reading,

00:28:25.003 --> 00:28:28.173
which is different than the way
we're educating people here.

00:28:28.173 --> 00:28:29.307
So look, I mean,

00:28:29.307 --> 00:28:30.842 align:start size:88% position:13%
mostly when I'm talking
about, you know,

00:28:30.842 --> 00:28:32.310
big tech or the term big tech,

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:34.746
I'm usually talking about
the censorship debate,

00:28:34.746 --> 00:28:36.414
which, you know,
I have views on.

00:28:36.414 --> 00:28:38.483
But that's not what I'm
gonna talk about here.

00:28:39.384 --> 00:28:40.652
This is a different issue

00:28:40.652 --> 00:28:44.222
and I think it's an
important one where,

00:28:45.523 --> 00:28:47.292
look, one of the
things I often say,

00:28:47.292 --> 00:28:49.160 align:start size:84% position:16%
not to get too
philosophical here,

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:50.628
but will just for a second,

00:28:50.628 --> 00:28:53.832 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that virtue is not a
product of capitalism, okay.

00:28:53.832 --> 00:28:58.803
Virtue is a pre-condition for
capitalism to do its work.

00:28:58.803 --> 00:29:02.474
And what I mean by
that is that, look,

00:29:04.309 --> 00:29:05.243 align:start size:88% position:13%
a similar way of saying
that is capitalism

00:29:05.243 --> 00:29:06.611
would be the perfect system

00:29:06.611 --> 00:29:08.880
for organizing a
society's affairs

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:12.650
if our wants actually
matched our needs.

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:15.053
And to the extent that
there's anything wrong

00:29:15.053 --> 00:29:18.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
with negative externalities
with capitalism,

00:29:18.189 --> 00:29:21.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
it can be explained, entirely,
I believe by the daylight

00:29:21.159 --> 00:29:22.627
between our wants and needs.

00:29:22.627 --> 00:29:24.963
And I think part of what the
stakeholder capitalism movement

00:29:24.963 --> 00:29:29.200
gets wrong is that it demands
of these capitalist actors

00:29:30.502 --> 00:29:32.270
the need to cultivate virtue

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:34.639 align:start position:0%
that should have been
cultivated through institutions

00:29:34.639 --> 00:29:37.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
that predate capitalism
or capital in the society.

00:29:37.008 --> 00:29:38.777 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The family, church.
- Families, perhaps? Right.

00:29:38.777 --> 00:29:41.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, religion,
whatever it is, you know.

00:29:41.379 --> 00:29:44.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
Where you pray,
national identity,
patriotism's at the level

00:29:44.282 --> 00:29:47.719 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the community, at the level
of the family, absolutely.

00:29:47.719 --> 00:29:49.487
So where do we see this
play out as it relates

00:29:49.487 --> 00:29:50.955
to tech companies and
where does the debate

00:29:50.955 --> 00:29:53.424 align:start position:0%
even get confused even by
conservatives over the last year

00:29:53.424 --> 00:29:56.427
for example, was when I saw the
story breakout by, you know,

00:29:56.427 --> 00:29:59.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Wall Street Journal doing
reporting that, you know,

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:02.433
Facebook knew that
the young teen girls

00:30:02.433 --> 00:30:06.037
who were using Instagram had
body image insecurity issues

00:30:06.037 --> 00:30:08.873 align:start size:88% position:13%
that were fueled and
made worse by Instagram,

00:30:08.873 --> 00:30:10.942
and they didn't do
anything about it.

00:30:10.942 --> 00:30:12.377
And by the way,

00:30:12.377 --> 00:30:16.181 align:start size:97% position:3%
they knew that they were
making anger management issues

00:30:16.181 --> 00:30:18.082
amongst their angry
users even worse,

00:30:18.082 --> 00:30:19.517
but didn't do anything about it.

00:30:19.517 --> 00:30:21.920 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so, you know, conservative
media called me to talk

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:25.423
about that issue 'cause
I had been a long critic

00:30:25.423 --> 00:30:27.559
of censorship by big
tech and said, okay,

00:30:27.559 --> 00:30:29.027
let's hit 'em hard.

00:30:29.027 --> 00:30:30.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
They knew and they didn't
do anything about it.

00:30:30.261 --> 00:30:31.529 align:start size:88% position:13%
And actually my view was
actually, no, no, no,

00:30:31.529 --> 00:30:33.398
you're getting this
all wrong, actually.

00:30:33.398 --> 00:30:36.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
We should not as a society
want Mark Zuckerberg

00:30:36.768 --> 00:30:40.872 align:start size:97% position:3%
or Jack Dorsey to be our
priests and rabbis to virtue.

00:30:40.872 --> 00:30:43.942 align:start position:0%
In fact, we should want a
society that cultivates the kind

00:30:43.942 --> 00:30:46.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
of self-confidence
among young teen girls

00:30:46.344 --> 00:30:49.714 align:start size:97% position:3%
that don't leave them
vulnerable to the insecurities

00:30:49.714 --> 00:30:53.017 align:start size:88% position:13%
that these algorithms
are designed to prey on.

00:30:53.017 --> 00:30:54.853
And I think that
we miss the fact

00:30:54.853 --> 00:30:58.289
that we can cultivate that
sense of psychic fortitude

00:30:58.289 --> 00:31:00.859
and virtue in the offline world

00:31:00.859 --> 00:31:03.928 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that these professionally
created algorithms

00:31:03.928 --> 00:31:05.897
aren't so well equipped to pick

00:31:05.897 --> 00:31:08.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
at and prey on those
insecurities by
knowing what you click

00:31:08.032 --> 00:31:09.534 align:start size:84% position:16%
on versus what you
don't click on and use

00:31:09.534 --> 00:31:12.203
that to create a window to
your soul that sees your soul

00:31:12.203 --> 00:31:14.405
with greater clarity
than you see yourself.

00:31:16.174 --> 00:31:19.210
That's just the antecedent
condition of the divergence

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:20.745
of your want and need,

00:31:20.745 --> 00:31:24.082
that deficit of virtue that
allowed the modern, demonic,

00:31:26.150 --> 00:31:28.987 align:start size:97% position:3%
algorithm empowered version
of modern capitalism

00:31:28.987 --> 00:31:31.022
and technology, the
industry part of it,

00:31:31.022 --> 00:31:32.490
to prey on those insecurities.

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:33.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's kind of like the
equivalent of a teen girl

00:31:33.992 --> 00:31:36.027
in the 1990s, you know,

00:31:36.027 --> 00:31:39.030 align:start position:0%
of Virginia Slims manufacturers
selling cigarettes

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:41.566 align:start size:97% position:3%
to that teen girl who had
body imaging security issues

00:31:41.566 --> 00:31:42.767
back then.

00:31:42.767 --> 00:31:44.002 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's the same thing
all over again

00:31:44.002 --> 00:31:45.403 align:start size:84% position:16%
when the thing we
need to be addressing

00:31:45.403 --> 00:31:47.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
is the generational
insecurity in the first place

00:31:47.705 --> 00:31:49.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
and cultivating that
psychic fortitude

00:31:49.841 --> 00:31:51.943 align:start position:0%
that actually allows
capitalism to then do its work,

00:31:51.943 --> 00:31:54.679 align:start size:84% position:16%
to sell us the goods
and services we want
more effectively

00:31:54.679 --> 00:31:57.482 align:start size:97% position:3%
when we already have a
deep seated self-confidence

00:31:57.482 --> 00:31:59.250
and understanding of
who we are as people

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:02.120 align:start size:88% position:13%
that predate our
identity as capitalists.

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:03.621
And so this is one of
the solutions I talk

00:32:03.621 --> 00:32:05.657
about in the book is, you
know, one of the places

00:32:05.657 --> 00:32:08.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
I wanna see the conservative
movement do better

00:32:08.159 --> 00:32:11.396 align:start size:97% position:3%
is to provide an affirmative
answer to those questions

00:32:11.396 --> 00:32:13.464
about identity, about
national identity,

00:32:13.464 --> 00:32:14.999
about individual identity,

00:32:14.999 --> 00:32:18.736 align:start size:84% position:16%
and to talk not just
about the revival

00:32:18.736 --> 00:32:20.305
of freedom through system

00:32:20.305 --> 00:32:21.539
or system of free
market capitalism

00:32:21.539 --> 00:32:22.407
where I'm one of the
greatest champions

00:32:22.407 --> 00:32:24.108
for exactly that model,

00:32:24.108 --> 00:32:27.245
but also to talk about
a revival of let's say,

00:32:27.245 --> 00:32:30.114
civic identity, our
identity as citizens,

00:32:30.114 --> 00:32:33.484 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the psychic fortitude
that we require as citizens.

00:32:33.484 --> 00:32:36.888
And the civic duty that comes
in our capacity as a citizen

00:32:36.888 --> 00:32:40.124
from our identity as just a
capitalist who's participant

00:32:40.124 --> 00:32:41.592
in that economy.

00:32:41.592 --> 00:32:44.329
And so what did big tech
do? They called that bluff.

00:32:44.329 --> 00:32:48.733
Modern social media called
the bluff of a generation

00:32:48.733 --> 00:32:51.102
that didn't know what
they actually wanted

00:32:51.102 --> 00:32:53.071 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then preyed on those
psychic insecurities

00:32:53.071 --> 00:32:54.906 align:start size:88% position:13%
to build multi-trillion
dollar companies

00:32:54.906 --> 00:32:56.307
that were not tech companies.

00:32:57.809 --> 00:33:01.279 align:start position:0%
They were psychologically
shrewd companies that understood

00:33:01.279 --> 00:33:02.747 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they could
understand you better

00:33:02.747 --> 00:33:04.949 align:start size:97% position:3%
with their algorithms than
you could understand yourself.

00:33:04.949 --> 00:33:08.553 align:start position:0%
And maybe if you understood
yourself even more effectively,

00:33:08.553 --> 00:33:10.555 align:start size:97% position:3%
that might have actually
prevented modern social media

00:33:10.555 --> 00:33:12.857 align:start size:97% position:3%
from having the toxic
effects that it's since had.

00:33:12.857 --> 00:33:15.493
- Vivek am I a crazy
conspiracy theorist

00:33:15.493 --> 00:33:17.795 align:start size:84% position:16%
or is it possible that
for all those years

00:33:17.795 --> 00:33:19.630
that Buzzfeed had
everyone clicking

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:22.567
on those nonsensical poles
of what do they like better,

00:33:22.567 --> 00:33:23.801
this or that,

00:33:23.801 --> 00:33:26.170
that perhaps that had
a little something,

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:28.006
Buzzfeed and many of these
other sites, but Buzzfeed,

00:33:28.006 --> 00:33:29.807 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is now
completely collapsed,

00:33:29.807 --> 00:33:31.909 align:start size:81% position:19%
was like the prime
purveyor of all

00:33:31.909 --> 00:33:33.644
of us clicking all these
things and they would,

00:33:33.644 --> 00:33:35.179
as you sort of alluded to there,

00:33:35.179 --> 00:33:39.317
ultimately they would know
what we actually want more

00:33:39.317 --> 00:33:41.686
than we can even understand.

00:33:41.686 --> 00:33:43.354 align:start size:94% position:6%
- That's not a conspiracy.
That's a reality.

00:33:43.354 --> 00:33:45.390
I mean, even look
at Mark Zuckerberg.

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:48.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, his genius was not
his technical aptitude.

00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:50.895
The predecessor to Facebook,

00:33:50.895 --> 00:33:53.064
I mean, I was at Harvard
with him at the same time.

00:33:53.064 --> 00:33:55.967
He was a year ahead of me
back then, was hot or not.

00:33:55.967 --> 00:33:57.802 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, this or this right.
(Dave laughs)

00:33:57.802 --> 00:34:00.905 align:start size:97% position:3%
Sort of taking these native
based human instincts

00:34:00.905 --> 00:34:04.475
and just picking at them and
exploiting them, you know.

00:34:04.475 --> 00:34:06.711
Now does that mean that we
should regulate the companies

00:34:06.711 --> 00:34:07.945
and prevent them
from doing that?

00:34:07.945 --> 00:34:09.680
No, no, no. You're
getting it all wrong.

00:34:09.680 --> 00:34:12.884
We need to revive the very
institutions at the level

00:34:12.884 --> 00:34:14.519
of the family, at the
level of the nation,

00:34:14.519 --> 00:34:16.020
at the level of the community,

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:18.256
at the level of even our
religious institutions,

00:34:18.256 --> 00:34:22.060 align:start size:97% position:3%
to fill that void of meaning
and purpose and identity.

00:34:22.060 --> 00:34:24.595
That then prepares
us for a society

00:34:24.595 --> 00:34:27.498 align:start position:0%
where in free market capitalism
we don't need to apologize

00:34:27.498 --> 00:34:29.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
for the fact that some guy
ends up with more green pieces

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:30.868
of paper than the other.

00:34:30.868 --> 00:34:33.938 align:start size:97% position:3%
Who cares if we fetishize
those green pieces of paper?

00:34:33.938 --> 00:34:36.507
The thing that really matters
is that we have civic equality

00:34:36.507 --> 00:34:37.442
as citizens.

00:34:37.442 --> 00:34:38.743
That we're grounded in the forms

00:34:38.743 --> 00:34:40.478
of identity that really matter.

00:34:40.478 --> 00:34:42.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's where I wanna see
the conservative movement

00:34:42.680 --> 00:34:45.650
do better in stepping up
to fill that void in a way

00:34:45.650 --> 00:34:47.852
that it's completely
abdicated any interest

00:34:47.852 --> 00:34:49.320
in actually taking on.

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:51.022 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's part of what motivated
me to say that, you know,

00:34:51.022 --> 00:34:53.157
I just wasn't done
with just Woke Inc.

00:34:53.157 --> 00:34:55.193
You know, Nation of Victims
was about taking a crack.

00:34:55.193 --> 00:34:56.694 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm not saying I
have all the answers,

00:34:56.694 --> 00:35:00.298
but taking a crack at what
fills that void of identity.

00:35:00.298 --> 00:35:04.802
What does it mean to be an
American in the year 2022?

00:35:04.802 --> 00:35:06.437
If we don't have a good
answer to that question,

00:35:06.437 --> 00:35:08.439
maybe we as a movement
need to be stepping up

00:35:08.439 --> 00:35:11.709
and answer that question or
else you're just gonna have

00:35:11.709 --> 00:35:14.112 align:start size:97% position:3%
that same poison continuing
to fill that black hole

00:35:14.112 --> 00:35:15.847
of a vacuum of identity,

00:35:15.847 --> 00:35:17.615
rather than just
taking a jackhammer

00:35:17.615 --> 00:35:20.318 align:start size:88% position:13%
to sort of weed out that
poison one at a time.

00:35:20.318 --> 00:35:22.587 align:start position:0%
Maybe you try a different
strategy of diluting the poison

00:35:22.587 --> 00:35:25.523
to irrelevance by filling
it with a new rich vision

00:35:25.523 --> 00:35:28.459
for what that identity could
be that dilutes the poison

00:35:28.459 --> 00:35:29.694
to irrelevance.

00:35:29.694 --> 00:35:31.496
That's the approach I'm
interested in taking.

00:35:31.496 --> 00:35:32.997 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So before we
finish with Strive,

00:35:32.997 --> 00:35:34.432 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is the culmination
of all of this,

00:35:34.432 --> 00:35:36.968
are you enthused or
do you see markers

00:35:36.968 --> 00:35:39.237
that some of what you're
calling for is happening?

00:35:39.237 --> 00:35:40.605
I mean, as you know,

00:35:40.605 --> 00:35:42.773
I toured with Jordan
Peterson for two years.

00:35:42.773 --> 00:35:46.611 align:start position:0%
The level of exactly what
you're asking for there in terms

00:35:46.611 --> 00:35:49.514 align:start size:97% position:3%
of personal responsibility,
family, belief,

00:35:49.514 --> 00:35:50.781
all of these things.

00:35:50.781 --> 00:35:52.283
I mean, he's spreading
that across the world.

00:35:52.283 --> 00:35:55.386
I see that coming now out of
other corners of conservatism.

00:35:55.386 --> 00:35:59.023 align:start position:0%
So it's not just, ah, government
off our back and low taxes.

00:35:59.023 --> 00:36:00.358 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's actually
something more robust.

00:36:00.358 --> 00:36:01.993
I see that kind of happening.

00:36:01.993 --> 00:36:03.961
Do you agree with that?

00:36:03.961 --> 00:36:05.429
- Yeah, I do.

00:36:06.597 --> 00:36:10.034
I do see a potential
shift in the tide.

00:36:10.034 --> 00:36:10.968
You know, it's interesting.

00:36:10.968 --> 00:36:12.770
One of the things I cover

00:36:14.005 --> 00:36:16.207
in Nation of Victims,
the second book is,

00:36:16.207 --> 00:36:17.942
is actually I take
a look at history,

00:36:17.942 --> 00:36:19.477
not just our national history.

00:36:19.477 --> 00:36:21.846 align:start size:97% position:3%
A lot of it goes into the
parts of the civil war history

00:36:21.846 --> 00:36:24.382 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I hadn't reminded
myself of since high school,

00:36:25.516 --> 00:36:26.951
but even through a
lot of Roman history.

00:36:26.951 --> 00:36:28.719 align:start position:0%
And one of the things I reminded
myself of is we often talk

00:36:28.719 --> 00:36:31.055 align:start position:0%
about the rise and fall of Rome
and then draw the analogies

00:36:31.055 --> 00:36:33.724
to the rise and fall of
the American experiment.

00:36:33.724 --> 00:36:36.294
And one of the things that
I reminded myself as part

00:36:36.294 --> 00:36:38.396
of the research for the
book, and it, you know,

00:36:38.396 --> 00:36:39.630 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's in a couple of
the chapters there now

00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:43.434 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that there was
no one fall of Rome.

00:36:43.434 --> 00:36:45.970 align:start size:84% position:16%
There were many
rises and many falls.

00:36:45.970 --> 00:36:47.071
And there were moments

00:36:48.506 --> 00:36:50.841
in what you thought might have
been the fall where the idea

00:36:50.841 --> 00:36:52.677 align:start size:81% position:19%
of Rome reinvented
itself, right?

00:36:52.677 --> 00:36:54.946
Not just the place,
Rome was an idea.

00:36:54.946 --> 00:36:56.814 align:start size:84% position:16%
In a certain sense,
America was that idea.

00:36:56.814 --> 00:36:58.816
Both were built on this
idea of exceptionalism.

00:36:58.816 --> 00:37:00.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
In a certain sense,
you can even...

00:37:03.054 --> 00:37:04.288
Not to get waxed too
philosophical about it,

00:37:04.288 --> 00:37:06.257
but maybe Rome was reborn.

00:37:06.257 --> 00:37:09.160
Maybe Rome was reborn in 1776.

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:11.596
And I think the idea
of unapologetically
leading in the way

00:37:11.596 --> 00:37:13.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the American
experiments sought to lead,

00:37:13.164 --> 00:37:14.632
these are ideas.

00:37:14.632 --> 00:37:16.667 align:start size:94% position:6%
Nations don't die the
way that human beings do.

00:37:16.667 --> 00:37:19.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're constantly
reborn as something else.

00:37:19.070 --> 00:37:21.005
I was actually raised
in a Hindu upbringing.

00:37:21.005 --> 00:37:23.674
We weaved some Hindu philosophy
into the book about the idea

00:37:23.674 --> 00:37:26.877 align:start size:97% position:3%
of individual reincarnation
into the reincarnation

00:37:26.877 --> 00:37:28.312
of the idea of a nation,

00:37:28.312 --> 00:37:30.448
the reincarnation
of America itself.

00:37:30.448 --> 00:37:31.716
You know what?

00:37:31.716 --> 00:37:33.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's not this
bilateral tug of war

00:37:33.117 --> 00:37:34.552
where the tides are changing,

00:37:34.552 --> 00:37:35.886 align:start size:97% position:3%
but rather if you look
through the arc of history,

00:37:35.886 --> 00:37:37.388
if you look through
the history of Rome,

00:37:37.388 --> 00:37:39.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then even look at the
history of this this country

00:37:39.223 --> 00:37:41.525 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we live in, that
we consider ours,

00:37:41.525 --> 00:37:44.028
it has an opportunity to
not just have one rise

00:37:44.028 --> 00:37:46.864
and one fall, but to
constantly be reborn,

00:37:46.864 --> 00:37:48.232
and to rediscover itself.

00:37:48.232 --> 00:37:50.101
And as there were many
rises and falls of Rome,

00:37:50.101 --> 00:37:52.503
I predict that there will
be many rises and falls

00:37:52.503 --> 00:37:55.740 align:start size:88% position:13%
of this version of Rome,
of America as well.

00:37:56.874 --> 00:37:58.609 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm optimistic that
we're not in...

00:37:58.609 --> 00:38:00.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're in what feels
like a decline,

00:38:00.177 --> 00:38:01.445 align:start size:81% position:19%
but is actually
just the beginning

00:38:01.445 --> 00:38:03.547
of a new revival.

00:38:03.547 --> 00:38:04.982
And, you know,

00:38:04.982 --> 00:38:06.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
I hope we each do our small
part in playing a role

00:38:06.817 --> 00:38:08.686
in making that happen.

00:38:08.686 --> 00:38:10.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
- There's that positivity
I'm always looking

00:38:10.454 --> 00:38:11.922
for on this show.

00:38:11.922 --> 00:38:14.825 align:start position:0%
So now bring us home with Strive
because you don't just talk

00:38:14.825 --> 00:38:16.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
about this stuff and just
write books about it.

00:38:16.894 --> 00:38:18.863
You actually do
real world stuff.

00:38:18.863 --> 00:38:21.899 align:start size:97% position:3%
And my audience is always
trying to figure out real ways

00:38:21.899 --> 00:38:24.135
to combat this nonsense.

00:38:24.135 --> 00:38:26.003
And I think most of my
audience has done a lot

00:38:26.003 --> 00:38:28.272
of the spiritual and
cultural work already.

00:38:28.272 --> 00:38:29.507
So they're ready for the,

00:38:29.507 --> 00:38:31.876 align:start size:88% position:13%
hey, what's the business
end of this thing?

00:38:31.876 --> 00:38:33.544
- Well, I think some of this
can be done through the market.

00:38:33.544 --> 00:38:35.780
I never imagined I was gonna
start another business again,

00:38:35.780 --> 00:38:37.181 align:start size:97% position:3%
but when I looked at myself
in the mirror and said,

00:38:37.181 --> 00:38:39.317
look, how are we gonna
address this problem?

00:38:39.317 --> 00:38:41.952 align:start size:84% position:16%
I really saw an
underutilized avenue

00:38:41.952 --> 00:38:43.321 align:start size:84% position:16%
to solve this
through the market.

00:38:43.321 --> 00:38:45.423
So Strive's a new
investment firm.

00:38:45.423 --> 00:38:47.358
We're competing with BlackRock.

00:38:47.358 --> 00:38:50.194
We're offering index funds,
just like BlackRock does.

00:38:50.194 --> 00:38:51.529
We listed our first index fund

00:38:51.529 --> 00:38:53.631
in the New York Stock
Exchange last month.

00:38:55.099 --> 00:38:57.802
But the key difference is
we bring a different voice

00:38:57.802 --> 00:39:00.471
and vote to the table
where we tell companies

00:39:00.471 --> 00:39:03.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
to focus exclusively on
delivering excellent products

00:39:03.541 --> 00:39:06.077 align:start position:0%
and services to their customers
over any other agenda,

00:39:06.077 --> 00:39:07.244
social or political agenda.

00:39:07.244 --> 00:39:08.412
Actually in Nation of Victims,

00:39:08.412 --> 00:39:09.347 align:start size:84% position:16%
the end of the
subtitle of the book

00:39:09.347 --> 00:39:11.315
is The Path Back to Excellence.

00:39:11.315 --> 00:39:13.751
I think part of the path
back to excellence runs

00:39:13.751 --> 00:39:15.186
through the market.

00:39:15.186 --> 00:39:16.454 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's actually what Strive
is all about as well.

00:39:16.454 --> 00:39:19.190
So just to give you
a tangible example.

00:39:19.190 --> 00:39:21.959 align:start size:97% position:3%
The first index fund we listed
is a US energy index fund.

00:39:21.959 --> 00:39:23.260
It trades on the New
York Stock Exchange.

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:24.762
Its ticker is DRILL.

00:39:24.762 --> 00:39:28.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's a very simple message
to the US energy industry.

00:39:28.733 --> 00:39:31.902
You should drill, frack,
do whatever allows you

00:39:31.902 --> 00:39:36.474
to be most successful without
having to apologize for it.

00:39:36.474 --> 00:39:37.742
If that's what allows
you to be successful

00:39:37.742 --> 00:39:39.210
over the long run,

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:40.010
you don't need to advance
somebody else's social

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:41.245
or political agenda.

00:39:41.245 --> 00:39:42.513
So that's why we pick,
you know, D-R-I-L-L.

00:39:42.513 --> 00:39:45.216
DRILL was to send a
very clear message.

00:39:45.216 --> 00:39:47.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I also wanna practice
what I'm preaching.

00:39:47.218 --> 00:39:48.686
And so, you know,

00:39:48.686 --> 00:39:52.089
that fund raised $300 million
in it's first three weeks,

00:39:52.089 --> 00:39:54.992
it's first month
or so of trading.

00:39:54.992 --> 00:39:56.827
But it's not just
about doing this

00:39:56.827 --> 00:39:58.262
for the sake of making a point,

00:39:58.262 --> 00:40:00.197 align:start position:0%
it's to bring a different
shareholder voice to the table.

00:40:00.197 --> 00:40:01.198
And I was alluding
to this earlier.

00:40:01.198 --> 00:40:02.633
That's why, you know,

00:40:02.633 --> 00:40:05.703 align:start size:88% position:13%
even in recent days sent
a letter to the board

00:40:05.703 --> 00:40:10.007
of Chevron questioning a lot
of their politicized behaviors

00:40:10.007 --> 00:40:12.943
that were influenced by
the likes of BlackRock.

00:40:12.943 --> 00:40:15.279
But the problem isn't just
as sit around and complain

00:40:15.279 --> 00:40:18.182
about this, well, let's bring
a different shareholder voice

00:40:18.182 --> 00:40:19.884
to the table into the boardroom,

00:40:19.884 --> 00:40:22.953
and actually go back
to resolving these
through free speech

00:40:22.953 --> 00:40:25.089
and open debate, both
in the public square

00:40:25.089 --> 00:40:26.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
as citizens on
political questions,

00:40:26.891 --> 00:40:28.659
and then back in the boardrooms.

00:40:28.659 --> 00:40:30.161
Bring real debate back
into the boardrooms.

00:40:30.161 --> 00:40:32.630 align:start size:84% position:16%
Bring actual
diversity of thought,

00:40:32.630 --> 00:40:34.165 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the name of diversity
that we've missed

00:40:34.165 --> 00:40:36.767
in corporate America, bring
actual diversity of thought

00:40:36.767 --> 00:40:38.335
back to the corporate boardroom

00:40:38.335 --> 00:40:40.771
to set this culture right
starting with our economy.

00:40:40.771 --> 00:40:43.207
So that's what I was doing
with Strive, but, you know,

00:40:43.207 --> 00:40:45.075 align:start position:0%
in some ways it's an embodiment
of what I wrote about not

00:40:45.075 --> 00:40:46.577
just in Woke Inc.,
which is the problem,

00:40:46.577 --> 00:40:48.012
but even in Nation of Victims

00:40:48.012 --> 00:40:50.314
and The Path back to
Excellence, you know,

00:40:50.314 --> 00:40:51.849
I think hopefully, you know,

00:40:51.849 --> 00:40:53.818
there's no end-all be-all
silver bullet solution,

00:40:53.818 --> 00:40:55.820
but I think market solutions
are gonna be an important part

00:40:55.820 --> 00:40:58.222
of it and that's where I
chose to focus my attention

00:40:58.222 --> 00:40:59.256
in the next chapter.

00:41:00.524 --> 00:41:01.392
- Love it, man.

00:41:01.392 --> 00:41:02.927
As simple as that.

00:41:02.927 --> 00:41:05.095
We will link to the book down
below and I'd keep you longer,

00:41:05.095 --> 00:41:07.798 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you're clearly a busy
guy and you got things to do.

00:41:07.798 --> 00:41:09.300
It was good to see you, man.

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:10.568 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Good to see you.
Thank you for having me.

00:41:10.568 --> 00:41:11.769
We'll see you.

00:41:11.769 --> 00:41:13.070 align:start size:84% position:16%
- If you're looking
for more honest

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:14.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
and thoughtful
conversations about politics

00:41:14.371 --> 00:41:15.773
instead of nonstop yelling,

